WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.390
Film Courage: Many new writers say that it’s
nearly impossible to get their scripts to

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.880
people that actually have the power to buy
them or green light them.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.509
That that’s their problem…that it’s
access.

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:16.369
And that’s the reason they’re not making
movies.

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:19.260
What are your thoughts on this?

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:27.050
CSUN Professor Eric Edson: I would say they
don’t know how lucky they are as of today.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.609
You have to get used to the system you have.

00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:34.770
For instance, back in the day (“back in
the day”), back in my day there was ONE

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.560
way into Hollywood, TV, Film…ONE way!

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.020
And that was through an agent’s front door.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.800
And that agent’s front door had layers and
layers of guardians before it.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.140
And that was also frustrating you know in
a similar way but very, very frustrating.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.460
But now what you have, you have a more creative
path.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.560
Let me put it this way.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.900
Contests…screenwriting contest have now
been around long enough to separate the wheat

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:06.900
from the chaff.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.390
You know, the good ones, year after year,
have gotten stronger because more people believe

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.380
in them and the less good ones (although they
are all sincere.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.690
I know they’re all sincere) have kind of
fallen by the wayside.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.410
But the test is this every time I hear that.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.810
I mean yes I have great sympathy for that,
I understand that.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.310
I remember when I was coming up myself and
it felt like and it was infuriating and frustrating,

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:41.340
but the number one reason why you are not
making it is the material is not yet good

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:47.610
enough because especially if you’re not
reading, you know, reading the stuff that’s

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:54.590
out there you have no way of knowing how good
good has to be in order to get noticed.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:02:01.220
And coming in, you have to be better than
most of the people who are now making a living

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.080
at it in order to get noticed.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:12.569
But the contests offer…I was talking about
reobjectification, the contests offer a concrete

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:14.160
way to do that.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.230
Work on it and work on it and work on it until
you’ve got something you are ready to test.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.840
But now, pick your contests carefully.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.600
The first time you try it out, pick smaller
contests, more fringing.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.650
I mean do your research, they should be decent,
upstanding contests and so forth.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:35.650
Try it out.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.410
Try it out.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.200
Did you make the quarterfinals?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.230
Or were you swept away in the first pass through?

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:49.660
Okay, if that happens, you have learned something,
it’s not ready.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.900
And you keep working on it and you keep working
on it.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:03:00.830
And finally when you get to the mid-level
ranks and let’s say your script gets to

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.700
the quarter final, okay.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.960
But it did not make the top ten or top 15,
fine.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.880
You’ve learned something more.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.940
Go back, take a vacation for a couple of weeks,
try not to think about it and read it again,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.130
make more notes and go back to work on it.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:27.060
And it’s a way, see that’s the thing,
what a lot of neophyte screenwriters, new

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:35.670
screenwriters don’t do or don’t pursue
which is, you’ve got to be as a writer as

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:47.950
a craftsperson, you must be relentless in
your goal of creating quality material.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:53.580
For instance (I’ll give you a for instance
and maybe that will sort it out), I’m working

00:03:53.580 --> 00:04:00.129
with a young lady right now who was one of
our grad students (two or three years ago).

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:04.709
She’s been my assistant, class assistant,
stuff like that, so we know each other pretty

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:05.910
well.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:13.980
She took her thesis screenplay from (I don’t
know) three years ago and I read it and I

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.260
told her “You’ve got a great idea here.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.799
You really do.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:19.430
I believe in this idea.”

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:24.460
And we had conversations about there’s no
such thing as a bad screenplay, only an unfinished

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:30.110
one and all of that and I gave her some pointers
and you know scribbled things on the pages

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.120
and she went back and she wrote it again.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.650
This is a feature film.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.110
And I looked at it and was “You know, this
is better.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.449
This is a little better.”

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:42.990
And I went through the sample editing and
scribble, scribble and all of that kind of

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:47.990
stuff and she takes it and she comes back
and it’s a little bit better.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:56.379
She has been through this process (well in
this case with me) it must be six times now.

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:59.129
I mean years have passed.

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:03.319
She had a draft of this when you went up for
her thesis three years ago, right?

00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:06.389
But I’ll tell you…I’m reading it once
again.

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:13.389
I’ll probably be looking at it again this
weekend, she is so close, so close!

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:19.539
I kept telling her, keep track of, don’t
lose your draft of your first draft.

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:21.990
Don’t lose a copy of your first draft.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.830
Because a day is coming, I would like to have
that in my hand and your final draft, put

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.330
that together in a binder and use this in
class, have people read the first draft and

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:38.330
now read the last draft and that I believe
could be a wonderful, wonderful exploration

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:44.529
of teaching and screenwriting because this
has become viable.

00:05:44.529 --> 00:05:53.560
She is now close to having an entirely shootable
casting really good social commentary screenplay.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:59.430
Film Courage: What changed in those 6 drafts
in the three years or however many?

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.110
Eric Edson: Craft.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.110
Craft.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:07.930
Her mastery of…I mean what most of them
do is they write what they mean when they

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:09.349
come to dialogue.

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:14.089
People just blurt it out and it’s called
on-the-nose dialogue.

00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:18.389
That’s one of the things you teach, stop
it!

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:20.110
People do not talk like that.

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:25.580
People do not talk around what they mean not
that there can’t be confrontation and stuff

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.039
like that under certain emotional circumstances,
yes.

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:38.899
But it was dialogue and less is more in terms
of the amount of dialogue, in description

00:06:38.899 --> 00:06:46.789
it was about the use of language and vocabulary
in description using irrelevant words The,

00:06:46.789 --> 00:06:51.300
And, There…there’s the list of the 9 most
utterly useless words.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.620
I got it back there on a sheet.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.830
I pass it out sometimes.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.120
All that is is filler.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.139
All that does it slow the reader down.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:09.539
It is developing a style in the way you describe
and offer exposition and description in scene

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:13.389
with scene heading and slug lines and then
what we’re looking at and stuff like that.

00:07:13.389 --> 00:07:21.069
Drawing people in, where you put things, building
the plot, in an ever better way you can do

00:07:21.069 --> 00:07:23.249
more here, you could do more here.

00:07:23.249 --> 00:07:25.509
That’s what has been going on for all those
years.

00:07:25.509 --> 00:07:29.580
Film Courage: So you liked her initial idea
of the story?

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.270
Eric Edson: Absolutely.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:35.529
And it was in very, rough form.

00:07:35.529 --> 00:07:36.619
Very rough form.

00:07:36.619 --> 00:07:39.330
She had written badly with pride.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:43.690
And underneath that there was just something
that really struck me.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:48.599
You know this is worth saying and this is
important to say.

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:50.520
It’s a social commentary piece.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.080
It’s not like there’s a big market out
there for social commentary pieces, there

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.080
isn’t.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:04.400
Okay, but it has to be done incredibly well
to catch the eyes you want to catch.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.419
Is it ever going to be made?

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:07.589
I don’t know if it’s ever going to be
made or not.

00:08:07.589 --> 00:08:10.599
That’s out of our hands.

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:13.430
But I am so proud of her.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:16.770
Boy, she has got a work sample for the ages.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:25.809
Film Courage: And you’ve let her know, not
too soon because once she takes this brilliant

00:08:25.809 --> 00:08:28.249
idea and it’s not ready, it could…

00:08:28.249 --> 00:08:29.919
Eric Edson: That’s the end of it.

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:32.270
That is correct, time and again.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.950
Oh she was planning two years ago to send
it off to one of the majors (I don’t know)

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.090
Scriptapalooza, one of the majors, you know.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:46.290
And she asked me to take one more look at
it before she sent it off and I said “I

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:51.470
think it’s self-defeating to send this off
yet.”

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.360
And there was a small contest.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:59.940
It’s the BFA (Broadcast Education Association)
has a student thing every year and they have

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.560
contests, a short film contests for students
and scripts and so forth.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.020
And I said “Try it there.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.020
Try it there.”

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:15.050
And she did and she learned some stuff, she
learned some stuff and she also learned that

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:19.950
sometimes judges can be jerks and that’s
part of the mix, too.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:26.650
But she learned, she kept working, she got
better.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.420
That is how you write.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:34.470
Everybody who says you’ve got…when I was
coming up…you’ve got to crank out a brand

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.530
new screenplay every six months.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:38.620
That is insane.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:44.120
I’m sorry, you can’t do good work if you’re
doing that.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.610
No, you have to live with it, you have to
grow with it, you have to be able to go back

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.590
and read it again and again and yet again.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:53.700
Drives my wife nuts.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.820
When I was working on the book [The Story
Solution: 23 Actions All Great Heroes Must

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:00.180
Take] and it was ready to send to the publisher.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.580
And she said “You’re sitting down to read
it again?

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.100
What is wrong with you?”

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.900
And I said “Honey, you married a mad man.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:09.700
I mean that goes with the territory.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.490
That’s what writers do.”

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:15.390
And I read it again and I found mistakes and
I fixed them.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.720
It’s endless.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:25.080
So the best thing for a writer is to have
been born a bit obsessive-compulsive.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:31.220
It’s like that is a trait that the craft
requires of us.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.240
You don’t just dash it off, you don’t.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:35.920
And do it well.

